00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:37.196
The microcosmos is not always a graceful space.

00:00:37.196 --> 00:00:43.770
Sometimes an organism just needs to get around
the way it gets around, even if that means

00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:49.570
looking like a swimming elephant head with
a truncated snout at one end and a rat tail

00:00:49.570 --> 00:00:51.071
at the other.

00:00:51.071 --> 00:00:58.210
Those odd creatures are called Bodonids, single-celled
eukaryotes with two flagella that create that

00:00:58.210 --> 00:00:59.870
frantic movement.

00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:06.270
They belong to a group of flagellated protists
called Kinetoplastida, and it was probably

00:01:06.270 --> 00:01:13.347
a relief to James, our master of microscopes,
that the Kinetoplastids he found had two flagella.

00:01:13.347 --> 00:01:18.870
If there was just one flagellum, he would
be dealing with the other half of the group:

00:01:18.870 --> 00:01:21.099
the Trypanosomatids.

00:01:21.099 --> 00:01:29.079
The family Trypanosomatida is made up entirely
of parasites that like to infect insects and plants.

00:01:29.079 --> 00:01:35.020
But some, like Trypanosoma cruzi, the organism
responsible for Chagas disease, can make their

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:41.579
way over to humans via their insect hosts,
causing aches and fever and swelling among

00:01:41.579 --> 00:01:44.500
a number of other symptoms.

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:51.227
There are some parasitic Bodonids, but those
ones tend to be found in fish and snails.

00:01:51.227 --> 00:01:56.829
And the family relationship they have with
Trypanosomatids has made Bodonids a handy

00:01:56.829 --> 00:02:03.000
organism to have around so that we can better
understand the diseases their relatives cause.

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:09.140
It’s a nice role for an organism that is
sometimes easy to miss against the backdrop

00:02:09.140 --> 00:02:11.025
of the microcosmos.

00:02:11.025 --> 00:02:17.080
Like if we hadn’t shown you what bodonids
look like already, and if we’d opened today’s

00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:19.140
episode with this scene...

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:21.796
would you know what you are looking for?

00:02:22.592 --> 00:02:28.680
I know I would probably have assumed they
were the green almond-shaped organisms, just

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:34.535
because their color and strange shape makes
them stand out so much more.

00:02:34.535 --> 00:02:40.349
And even in this scene, where there are so
many more bodonids to see, they’re just

00:02:40.349 --> 00:02:46.253
so secondary to the giant rotifer picking
away at their population.

00:02:46.253 --> 00:02:51.880
Those other creatures are fascinating and
we’ve encountered them plenty of times on

00:02:51.880 --> 00:02:54.447
our journey through the microcosmos.

00:02:54.447 --> 00:03:00.191
But you know what they don’t have that Bodonids
and their fellow kinetoplastids do?

00:03:00.987 --> 00:03:01.974
Chainmail.

00:03:06.157 --> 00:03:07.691
Yes, chainmail.

00:03:07.691 --> 00:03:11.008
But also fine, not actual chainmail.

00:03:11.008 --> 00:03:14.703
Bodonids aren’t decorated in metallic loops.

00:03:14.703 --> 00:03:20.620
What they have is actually something more
unique and fascinating—something that has

00:03:20.620 --> 00:03:26.512
changed the way we see the molecules and processes
fundamental to life.

00:03:26.512 --> 00:03:31.193
Kinetoplastids get their name from this unique
chainmail structure.

00:03:31.193 --> 00:03:35.819
It’s called the kinetoplast, and it’s
found inside their mitochondrion.

00:03:35.819 --> 00:03:40.262
The kinetoplast is unique, but its building
blocks aren’t:

00:03:40.262 --> 00:03:42.000
it’s just DNA.

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:48.220
You know, that double helix found in all of
us, that molecule that holds the instructions

00:03:48.220 --> 00:03:51.126
for how our cells look and behave.

00:03:51.126 --> 00:03:56.700
When scientists were finally able to see the
kinetoplast under an electron microscope,

00:03:56.700 --> 00:04:01.319
they realized that the DNA inside of it does
something kind of funny.

00:04:01.319 --> 00:04:09.860
It forms thousands and thousands of tiny little
circles, along with around 25 to 50 larger circles.

00:04:09.860 --> 00:04:16.442
And those circles of DNA link together, building
a structure that, in many kinetoplastids,

00:04:16.442 --> 00:04:18.645
looks a lot like chainmail.

00:04:18.645 --> 00:04:23.341
Scientists have done all sorts of experiments
to learn more about this DNA.

00:04:23.341 --> 00:04:28.967
But one of the most important things they’ve
learned isn’t about DNA—it’s about DNA’s

00:04:28.967 --> 00:04:31.200
single-stranded cousin RNA.

00:04:31.949 --> 00:04:37.957
We said earlier that DNA is like the instruction
manual that tells our cells what to do.

00:04:37.957 --> 00:04:43.630
But an instruction manual is just a stack
of paper if no one is around to read it.

00:04:43.630 --> 00:04:49.410
So inside of cells, there is a mechanism in
place to read that DNA, transcribing it into

00:04:49.410 --> 00:04:53.758
a single-stranded genetic material called
messenger RNA.

00:04:53.758 --> 00:04:59.759
You can think of that RNA as like someone
reading aloud from the instruction manual.

00:04:59.759 --> 00:05:06.090
After, that messenger RNA’s words get translated
into action, producing a protein that can

00:05:06.090 --> 00:05:09.366
go on to serve some function in the cell.

00:05:09.366 --> 00:05:15.951
That process—DNA transcribed to RNA and
then translated into protein—forms what

00:05:15.951 --> 00:05:20.461
many of us know as the central dogma of biology.

00:05:20.461 --> 00:05:23.380
It is beautiful, it is elegant.

00:05:23.380 --> 00:05:30.165
And it is also far too simplistic for what
life actually looks like.

00:05:30.165 --> 00:05:37.509
There are many ways that process is more complicated
than what we originally said, but we’re

00:05:37.509 --> 00:05:42.383
here for the kinetoplastids and their strange
DNA chain mail.

00:05:42.383 --> 00:05:47.970
One of the unusual things researchers in the
1980s noticed as they studied kinetoplast

00:05:47.970 --> 00:05:54.660
DNA is that there were genes that almost coded
for things we knew they should code for.

00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:59.630
The sequences in them created instructions
that looked like they should make a particular

00:05:59.630 --> 00:06:04.980
protein found in these organisms, but something
would be off.

00:06:04.980 --> 00:06:10.850
Mutations would shift the instructions slightly,
almost as if it were missing key words or

00:06:10.850 --> 00:06:14.475
someone had deleted a step in the instruction
manual.

00:06:14.475 --> 00:06:16.900
But that was in the DNA.

00:06:16.900 --> 00:06:22.800
When they studied the RNA, the instructions
were suddenly correct again, almost as if

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:25.684
they had been edited to make sense.

00:06:25.684 --> 00:06:30.820
In fact, that’s what the researchers would
call the process: RNA editing.

00:06:30.820 --> 00:06:35.800
This process turned up over and over again
in the kinetoplastids.

00:06:35.800 --> 00:06:41.776
Nonsensical instructions in the DNA would
make sense later thanks to RNA editing, a

00:06:41.776 --> 00:06:48.567
biological process poking at the person reading
from the manual to correct their final message.

00:06:48.567 --> 00:06:54.420
Kinetoplasts aren’t the only organisms to
use RNA editing, and it’s not quite clear

00:06:54.420 --> 00:06:57.709
what advantages there are to the process.

00:06:57.709 --> 00:07:01.817
Perhaps it allows a single gene to encode
multiple proteins.

00:07:01.817 --> 00:07:06.246
Perhaps it’s a necessary adaptation to contend
with mutations.

00:07:06.246 --> 00:07:12.970
Or perhaps it’s some other explanation we
don’t even know enough to imagine now.

00:07:12.970 --> 00:07:18.573
Bodonids are not the only microbes that force
us to reevaluate the fundamentals.

00:07:18.573 --> 00:07:23.010
Microbes are constantly showing us different
ways to do the basics,

00:07:23.010 --> 00:07:26.060
 and inspiring new techniques in the process.

00:07:26.060 --> 00:07:33.039
Tools like CRISPR are rooted in this kind
of reevaluation, this expansion and complication

00:07:33.039 --> 00:07:35.017
of our dogmas.

00:07:35.017 --> 00:07:41.170
This is usually the point in an episode where
I want to end by sighing and saying,

00:07:41.170 --> 00:07:44.850
 “All that, and this organism is just living its
life!

00:07:44.850 --> 00:07:46.240
It doesn’t know!”

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:49.379
Because that is sometimes what it feels like.

00:07:49.379 --> 00:07:56.774
Hundreds of words about this invisible process
buried deep in this funny, flagellated creature

00:07:56.774 --> 00:08:00.188
that doesn’t know what we’re even talking
about.

00:08:00.188 --> 00:08:05.633
It doesn’t know why we’ve spent all this
time looking at it and studying it.

00:08:05.633 --> 00:08:08.714
And even if it did, would it really care?

00:08:08.714 --> 00:08:14.630
It has a whole life to live, a life that will
continue long after I’m done talking about

00:08:14.630 --> 00:08:16.198
RNA editing.

00:08:16.198 --> 00:08:21.050
And we will never get to know what the entirety
of that life is.

00:08:21.050 --> 00:08:27.550
Only that it involves chainmail made of DNA,
circles of information linked together to

00:08:27.550 --> 00:08:30.219
produce questions we will chase forever.

